PERL 4.036 ON OS-9/68000========================Larry Wall's Perl 4, patch level 36, from CPAN (perl-4.036.tar.gz), builtwith Microware cc for OS-9/68000.  osk.diff is every change from the CPANsource, as a unified diff; the rest of this file says why.BUILDING    perl|perl4|<22 sources>|CPP2 MEM=128k|unix.l blarslib.l mytime.r math.l sys.lis its line in the collection's rebuild recipes.  CPP2 is needed becauseperl's macros run past what Microware's cpp will take.  unix.l must comebefore blarslib.l, or their two isatty psects collide at link time.perly.c and perly.h are yacc's output for perly.y, made on another system,because this disk has no yacc that takes perl's grammar.WHAT OS-9 CHANGESconfig.h was written by hand.  Configure is a Bourne shell script, andevery switch in it was decided from a link probe against the librariesnamed above.Processes.  OS-9 has no fork(): F$Fork starts a new process from a module.osk.c builds system(), exec, `backticks` and piped opens on os9exec() andwait(), the way Bob Larson's blarslib popen does.  A command line goes to$SHELL as a single argument, which is how Microware's shell is invoked;without SHELL set, perl forks `shell'.  exec runs the program, waits, andexits with its status, because a chained perl would lose its open paths.fork itself is reported as unsupported.Errors.  $! reads its text from /dd/SYS/errmsg, which has one line perOS-9 error number; without that file it gives the number.Files.  stat() fills st_mode with OS-9's attribute byte, so perl.hredefines S_IFMT and S_IFDIR to match, and -d and -f work.  A script's/dev/null opens /nil.  An SCF path such as /nil or /term has no filedescriptor to stat, so a successful open of one is taken as a characterdevice instead of failing.Sorting.  Microware's qsort finds its pivot by scanning with no bound, andtrusts the comparison to answer 0 for an element against itself.  A sortsubroutine such as { -1 } never does, and the scan runs off the end of thearray.  osk.c supplies a Shell sort that compares only elements it holds.Numbers.  Perl prints a number with %.20g.  clib turns the digits past theseventeenth into noise, so 18 printed as 18.000000000000000444; OS-9 perluses %.15g, which is what a double holds.atan2.  blarslib's atan2 returns the high half of its double as an int;osk.c computes it from atan.Smaller things: toke.c drops `case '\r'' because OS-9 C's '\n' is CR andthe two cases collided; cmd.c is built with CRIPPLED_CC, perl's own switchfor compilers that run out of temporaries; cmd.h's curcsv loses aninitialiser Microware cc cannot place.A2Px2p/ is the kit's awk-to-perl translator, built as CMDS/a2p with its ownrecipe line.  x2p/a2p.c is a2p.y run through this disk's own yacc, witha2py.c (which a2p.y includes) written in, because the build driver findsmain() by file; a2p.y and a2py.c are here as they came.  Two changes:a2p.h's ops table, 200K as a static array, pushed every global after itout of reach of the 16-bit data offsets Microware cc uses, so mainallocates it; and two of its globals are marked EXT, which the linkerhere needs.  config.h's mappings onto osk.c are perl's alone, and a2p'srecipe defines A2P to leave them out.  s2p is a Bourne shell script andfind2perl emits Unix find semantics, so neither is here.WHAT DOES NOT WORKfork, pipe with fork, crypt, sockets, links, symlinks, rmdir, chroot,getppid and the password and group functions.  Each one reports"Unsupported function" or fails with $! set.TESTSperl's own t/ suite, run file by file on this build: most files passcompletely.  The failures that remain want a program the disk does nothave (/bin/rm, ./perl, tr, ln, touch) or a service from the list above.